## keel

This repository is run by keel: a human Board, the keel Steward (deterministic code) and five LLM seats
(product, architect, planner, engineer, reviewer). Work is contract-bound: every seat gets a compiled,
hashed brief, and the Steward checks everything a seat hands in.

### If keel dispatched you

`KEEL_RUN_ID` is set in your environment and your first message names a brief.

1. Your brief is your whole contract. Read it once, top to bottom. To see it again, run
   `keel brief <subject> --seat <seat>` or use the MCP tool `keel_context`.
2. ACK before your first edit, on the submit channel the brief names: `keel api ack --input - --json`, the
   structured final message, or the MCP tool `keel_submit`.
3. Ask, record rulings and submit your result on the same channel (`keel api ask|rule|submit`).
4. Follow the keel skill for your seat: `keel-frame` (product), `keel-design` (architect), `keel-plan`
   (planner), `keel-work` (engineer), `keel-review` (reviewer).

### Rules for every agent in this repository

- Precedence: Board ruling > invariant > accepted ADR obligation > frozen intent > requirement > plan >
  task notes > model preference.
- Four stop classes always go to the Board as an ask: irreversible or destructive operations,
  security-sensitive actions, side effects outside the workspace, and every path forward being a guess.
- Do not commit, push, rebase, move refs or write commit trailers; the Steward makes every commit.
- Do not run `keel new`, `keel run`, `keel land`, `keel sync`, `keel audit` or `keel approve`. They refuse
  inside a keel run, and approvals come only from a Board member at an interactive terminal.
- Do not edit `.keel/**` or the files listed in `.keel/generated.lock.json` by hand. Governance files
  change only through a Board signature; living specs and the architecture model change only at land.
- Never open provider, credential or shared runtime settings files, and never print environment values.
  keel stores environment variable names only. On an authentication error, ask; do not look for keys.
- Use no permission-bypass flags and spawn no writing subagents.

### If you are working interactively

Run `keel status --next` to see the legal next actions, and `keel trace <file:line>` to see which goal,
requirement and approval a line serves. The Board's guide is `docs/00a-owner-guide.md` in the keel
package.

